<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  TC8 §4.8.6.6 TCP_FLAGS_INVALID_03: TCP, in SYN-SENT state, MUST
  ignore a segment carrying both ACK and RST flags with an
  unacceptable ACK number. Derived from RFC 793 §3.9 p66 Event
  Processing (MUST).

  v3.0 Test Procedure (p328):
    1. TESTER: Cause DUT to move on to SYN-SENT state
    2. TESTER: Send a segment having both ACK and RST flags with
       unacceptable ACK number
    3. DUT:    Do not send any response
    4. TESTER: Verify that the DUT remains in SYN-SENT state

  Observable protocol exchange (active OPEN to an unbound tester
  port + ISN_d learn + spec-asserted ACK+RST + 3 s absence window):
    a. The tester suppresses its kernel auto-RST so the DUT-emitted
       SYN to the unbound tester port does not provoke a RST that
       would race-kill the DUT's SYN-SENT before the ACK+RST inject
       lands.
    b. DUT performs an active OPEN on (kBasicsActiveLocalPort,
       kBasicsActiveRemotePort) — DUT issues SYN, enters SYN-SENT.
    c. The DUT-emitted pure SYN is observed to learn ISN_d.
    d. tester injects ACK+RST with seq=kTesterInitialSeq and
       ack=ISN_d + kUnacceptableAckOffset (NOT in [SND.UNA, SND.NXT]
       = {ISN_d + 1}, so unacceptable per RFC 793 §3.4).
    e. SCXML phase 1 (listening_dut_syn) confirms DUT-side SYN-SENT
       entry by observing the DUT-emitted pure SYN on the active
       4-tuple — the positive trigger keeps the absence window
       conditional on the prelude actually reaching SYN-SENT.
    f. SCXML phase 2 (listening_absence) waits 3 s for any DUT-
       emitted RST or pure ACK on the same 4-tuple. Both would be
       wire-observable spec violations:
         * DUT-emitted RST: DUT mishandled the ACK+RST as a
           connection request and emitted its own RST.
         * DUT-emitted pure ACK: DUT issued a challenge-ACK
           response, also a violation of the strict "ignore" mandate.
       DUT SYN retransmits (~1 s cadence) are tolerated — the SYN
       bit is set, so they match neither is_pure_dut_ack nor
       is_dut_rst. DUT remaining in SYN-SENT throughout the absence
       is the spec's expected steady state.

  ## DUT dispatch behaviour

  In SYN-SENT the DUT checks the ACK first. For an ACK that is not
  in the open half-window, the DUT reaches the RST branch which
  discards the segment WITHOUT emitting any response (no challenge-
  ACK path at this state — RFC 793 §3.9 p66 conformant). DUT remains
  in SYN-SENT and SYN retransmit cadence continues normally.

  The auto-RST suppression covers the spec-asserted exchange only.
  Subsequent SYN retransmits afterward draw a tester RST+ACK with
  ack=ISN_d+1 (acceptable), which the DUT processes as RFC 793 §3.9
  RST+acceptable-ACK → CLOSED. DUT silently transitions; no
  DUT-emitted RST or pure ACK results, so the absence guard remains
  satisfied. The closed-via-tester-RST tail is not part of the spec
  assertion — the assertion fires on the spec-injected ACK+RST,
  whose response (or lack thereof) the absence window catches.

  ## Port-quad selection — kBasicsActiveLocalPort + 20 / +20

  Cluster regression on a single worker netns shares state across
  cases. FLAGS_INVALID_15's phases use offsets +0..+6, and tester-
  side TCP state lingers on those tuples (TIME-WAIT, half-orphan
  listeners) for tens of seconds after 15 finishes. A fresh 03
  active OPEN to (+0) on the same worker draws a stray ACK from the
  leftover state, which the SYN-SENT DUT answers with RFC 793 §3.4
  RST(seq=incoming.ack), false-firing the absence guard. Choosing
  +20 (well clear of 15's +0..+6 range) eliminates the cross-case
  contamination — see reference_active_open_port_quad_collision.md
  for the BASICS_11 incident the pattern was first documented in.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       xmlns:sce="http://sce.dev/ext"
       xmlns:cpp="urn:sce:cpp"
       version="1.0"
       name="tcp_flags_invalid_03"
       datamodel="null"
       initial="listening_dut_syn">

  <sce:context id="captured"
               cpp:type="tc8::TcpCaptured"
               cpp:include="sce_integration/tcp_captured.h"/>

  <sce:context id="expected"
               cpp:type="tc8::Ipv4Expected"
               cpp:include="sce_integration/ipv4_expected.h"/>

  <state id="listening_dut_syn">
    <onentry>
      <send event="deadline_dut_syn" delay="5s"/>
    </onentry>
    <transition event="tcp_observed"
                cond="cpp:captured.is_dut_syn(expected.dut_iface_ip, expected.tester_ip, ::tc8::sce::tcp::kBasicsActiveLocalPort + 20U, ::tc8::sce::tcp::kBasicsActiveRemotePort + 20U)"
                target="listening_absence"/>
    <transition event="deadline_dut_syn" target="fail_no_dut_syn"/>
  </state>

  <state id="listening_absence">
    <onentry>
      <send event="deadline_absence" delay="3s"/>
    </onentry>
    <transition event="tcp_observed"
                cond="cpp:captured.is_dut_rst(expected.dut_iface_ip, expected.tester_ip, ::tc8::sce::tcp::kBasicsActiveLocalPort + 20U, ::tc8::sce::tcp::kBasicsActiveRemotePort + 20U) or captured.is_pure_dut_ack(expected.dut_iface_ip, expected.tester_ip, ::tc8::sce::tcp::kBasicsActiveLocalPort + 20U, ::tc8::sce::tcp::kBasicsActiveRemotePort + 20U)"
                target="fail_unexpected_response"/>
    <transition event="deadline_absence" target="pass"/>
  </state>

  <final id="pass">
    <donedata>
      <content>{"verdict":"pass"}</content>
    </donedata>
  </final>

  <final id="fail_no_dut_syn">
    <donedata>
      <content>{"verdict":"fail","reason":"no_dut_syn_to_active_open"}</content>
    </donedata>
  </final>

  <final id="fail_unexpected_response">
    <donedata>
      <content>{"verdict":"fail","reason":"dut_emitted_response_to_ack_rst_with_unacceptable_ack"}</content>
    </donedata>
  </final>
</scxml>
